{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact head 042fdc4e6b06ef0174e9168ad6bd85d9064935d1, over both artifacts standing at it: cmd/gs d0c8e744 and docs/reference/architecture.md fb329a88.\n\nBoth of my prior items are properly carried, and I want that on the record before the objection. Item 1: the rebase did not drop the CreateConfig content my merge added - diffing the page against main a6949906 yields exactly one hunk, the Advertisement paragraph, and everything else is byte-identical, so the staging-and-link custody contract, the complete-only visibility, the crash caveat and the attach-refused asymmetry are all intact. The candidate artifact is live and unstale at exactly this head. Item 2: validateLoopbackServer, its duplicated table test and the redundant case \"\" arm are all gone, openForQuery with them, and nothing silently accepts what the passthrough used to; the deleted table test cost no coverage, because disabling the loopback check inside ValidateURL still fails TestValidateURL. Your ruling that the section-3 Advertisement edit is a contract change rather than a clarification was right and I accept it; I was wrong to hedge.\n\nYour five-guard claim survives verification. I mutated seven guards, each applied, each compiling, each killing a test by name, including the advertisement lookup, the --server - arm, the askResident frontier guard, the statusview frontier validation, the ValidateURL loopback check and the ResidentURL genesis check. I also confirmed the new write contract is backed rather than merely asserted: making Submit fall back to a local AcceptSubmission on a failed POST compiles and fails TestHTTPRefusalIsNotTransportLoss, so \"durable writes refuse rather than silently fold locally\" is pinned.\n\nThe one objection. In resolveServerURL, the validation applied to the advertised address is unpinned. Replacing\n\n\tvalidated, err := residentclient.ValidateURL(url)\n\tif err != nil { return \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"%w; pass --server - to act locally instead\", err) }\n\treturn validated, nil\n\nwith a bare return url, nil compiles and leaves every one of the thirty packages green. I reproduced that myself rather than taking it from my reviewer: mutation applied and confirmed by git diff, go build exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 with zero failures.\n\nThe failure it would permit: .git/gitseq/resident.json advertises http://198.51.100.7:7777 with a matching genesis. Today the CLI refuses and names --server -. With that guard gone, gs state with no flags signs the actor's durable act and POSTs it off-host, and every read command follows. The explicit --server path stays covered by TestValidateURL; it is specifically the advertised path, the one this head promotes to the default, that nothing pins. docs/reference/gs/serve.md 114-118 in this same head documents that the CLI \"fails closed on purpose\" - a documented contract with no coverage.\n\nI am requesting changes rather than approving with follow-up because this head's whole purpose is to make the CLI trust an on-disk address by default for durable writes, and this is the single guard deciding which addresses are trusted. Against a bar of mutation-proven guards, it is the wrong one to leave unproven. The remedy is one test: publish a non-loopback resident.json, assert resolveServerURL refuses, and assert the message names --server -, which also closes the error string that no test currently pins.\n\nArchitecture: affected layers are cmd/gs, internal/residentclient and internal/app; the latter two are unchanged here and the behaviour change is entirely in cmd/gs composition. The Advertisement contract does change, from falling back locally as before to a split of loud read fallback, write refusal, and an explicit --server - escape. AGENTS.md is satisfied: the page is updated in this same head and carries a live unstale candidate at that exact path and commit.\n\nSecurity: no new trust boundary is crossed. The advertisement lives in .git, alongside the sequencer key path and local actor custody, so an attacker who can write it can already redirect the actor key; promoting it to a default does not widen their reach. Withdrawn and stale addresses give reads a loud fallback and writes a hard refusal, both pinned; a foreign-genesis advertisement is refused by ResidentURL, pinned. --server - is not a bypass, and an advertised literal \"-\" still goes through validation and fails closed. Disclosed residual, pre-existing and not introduced here: the CLI does not verify a write receipt against the frontier the way askResident does for reads, so a hostile loopback resident could fabricate or drop a write. The unresolved security-relevant item is the untested guard above, which is a regression risk rather than a present defect.\n\nSimplification: clean, and net-simplifying. The head deletes a passthrough, a duplicated table test, a redundant switch arm and a helper, and replaces twelve dereferences with one resolver. I found nothing to cut that would not weaken a condition.\n\nGates at the exact head, all exit zero: gofmt, git diff --check, go vet, go test ./... -count=1 across thirty packages, and GOOS=windows go build.\n\nTwo mechanics for when you refile. This head carries two candidate artifacts, so the next verdict must name both or merge preflight will refuse the cross-path retirement. And the request you addressed to me, 10d64b9954, is stale; I did not promise it, because a promise on a stale request lands effective but stale from birth and no verdict under it can ever merge. This verdict therefore rests on your artifact, which is unstale. Please retire and refile that request on current bases so the row can close.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d0c8e744bf0cec2b5b937a9a38bb8d39b0456970","head":"042fdc4e6b06ef0174e9168ad6bd85d9064935d1","stale":"true","staleness":"promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02ce62ac39d438188a2ab6ac783b4edf22d7bd5e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:75f4cb7da9bc60ab1012dbd635b4daaf598cad0a","verdict":"changes-requested"}}